.TH CCHAN_SELECTABLE 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cchan_selectable \- resolve a cchan into a cselectable for the calling thread and requested direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

cselectable cchan_selectable(cchan *ch, cselect_dir dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A cchan is made of two circular queues. This function looks at
the ID of the calling thread and at the direction you ask for, and
picks which of the two queues to wrap:

  cselect_read; owner <- workers_to_owner_cq  (same as cchan_recv)
                    worker <- owner_to_workers_cq
  cselect_write; owner -> owner_to_workers_cq  (same as cchan_send)
                    worker -> workers_to_owner_cq

The queue is picked here, at the call site, so cselect() never
needs to look inside the opaque cchan struct. The cchan
pointer is not kept: The returned cselectable holds only the
ccircq pointer that was picked and the dir value, and it
watches that one queue whichever thread later waits on it or runs a
callback for it.

cselect() and ceventloop only report that the queue is ready,
and the caller does the receive or the send itself.

When cselect() returns on the thread that built the selectable,
cchan_try_recv() and cchan_try_send() pick the same
queue, and so do ccircq_try_recv() and ccircq_try_send()
on sel.cq.

Inside a ceventloop callback, use ccircq_try_recv() and
ccircq_try_send() on sel->cq and never a cchan_* call. The
callback runs on a thread of the loop, which the channel treats as a
worker no matter which thread built the selectable, so when the owner
built it, a cchan_* call in the callback reaches the other queue.
Take a selectable that the owner built for cselect_read, which
watches workers_to_owner_cq: A cchan_try_recv() inside
on_readable receives from owner_to_workers_cq instead. It takes a
message that the owner meant for its workers, and leaves the message
that triggered the callback in its queue.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to resolve.  Must not be NULL.
dir  cselect_read or cselect_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A cselectable of type cselectable_type_ccircq that wraps the right
ccircq for the calling thread and for the direction.

If ch is NULL, the result is a cselectable_type_ccircq with a NULL
queue pointer, which cselect() refuses with ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The direction is decided once, when this function is called, and not
again on each pass of cselect(). If the calling thread's role
changes between building the selectable and the actual cselect()
call, build the selectable again.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
The function reads ch->owner_tid and two pointer fields, which are
written once when the cchan is created and never change after
that, so it is safe to call from any thread at the same time as any
other call.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* The owner watches the replies of its workers on a ceventloop. */
static void on_reply(ceventloop loop, ceventloop_reg reg,
                     cselectable *sel, void *arg) {
    (void)loop; (void)reg;
    cmessage msg;
    /* sel->cq, and not cchan_try_recv(): this runs on a thread of
       the loop, which the channel counts as a worker. */
    while (ccircq_try_recv(sel->cq, &msg) == ccol_success)
        handle_reply(arg, &msg);
}

ceventloop_handlers h = { .on_readable = on_reply };
ceventloop_reg reg = ceventloop_add(
    loop, cchan_selectable(ch, cselect_read), h, ctx, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cselect (3),
.BR ceventloop_add (3),
.BR ccircq_try_recv (3),
.BR ccircq_try_send (3),
.BR cchan_recv (3),
.BR cchan_send (3)
